   FILE:LINE: `scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh:985-989`, `scripts/install.sh:1914-1916`, `scripts/upgrade.sh:3305-3307,3355-3361`, `scripts/tests/test-night-mode-ignore-effect.sh:229-244`  
   Concrete failure scenario: when `.claude/.gitignore` is absent, `_apply_claude_dir_gitignore` creates it and returns before the effective-ignore and tracked-file checks. A user-ceremony adopter who already committed `.claude/settings.local.json` therefore gets a successful install/upgrade while that posture file remains tracked and commit-eligible. User ceremony skips the root helper that would otherwise detect it. The efficacy test always commits the baseline before creating the artifacts, so it only tests untracked files.  
   Minimal fix: continue through both `_gitignore_reassert_effective` calls after creation. Add fresh-user install and pre-state-user upgrade controls with an absent nested ignore and an already-tracked overlay.

2. SEVERITY: P1 — fix before rc.4  
   FILE:LINE: `scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh:1032-1073`, `scripts/install.sh:1790-1805,1860-1870,1899-1905`, `scripts/upgrade.sh:3327-3335,3355-3358`, `.claude/scripts/local/release.sh:103`  
   Concrete failure scenario: dry-run never checks tracked sensitive paths. With complete, effective ignore rules but a tracked overlay, nested dry-run reports `would PRESERVE`; root dry-run reports `would ENSURE`. Real execution fails and demands `git rm --cached`. This contradicts the signed-tag headline’s “honest dry-run previews.”  
   Minimal fix: share a read-only tracked-path classifier between apply and preview for root and nested surfaces, reporting that the real run would refuse. Add tracked-path dry-run controls.

3. SEVERITY: P2 — follow-up  
